  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "Direct hit on the scenario: it gives honest partial-conformance wording, separates protocol-boundary app identity from local IDs and host behavior, and makes provisional versus blocked claims auditable with status tags and evidence refs.",
    "strengths": [
      "Three separate claim cards sharply distinguish port/runtime scope, app semantics, and host-local dependencies.",
      "The App Semantics Card directly answers the scenario's need for honest wording around supported operations, local IDs, signature carriage, and capability/witness language.",
      "The local promise-accounting ledger improves auditability for availability claims, failed retrievals, break-witness, effects, snapshots, and key rotation."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Mandatory field semantics and a machine-checkable schema are still open, so different implementations could produce uneven or partly ambiguous cards.",
      "app_identity_at_protocol_boundary and related contract references point in the right direction but do not yet settle the authoritative cross-implementation identity rule."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Useful provisional card language could ossify into a de facto standard before DR/DI or frozen specs ratify it.",
      "Evidence refs and migration notes may remain too free-form for strong automated comparison across implementations."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Which claim-card fields and ledger rows must be mandatory for minimum honest app-semantic conformance?",
      "What exact artifact anchors authoritative protocol-boundary app identity across implementations and key rotation?",
      "Which signature-carriage and capability/witness statements are safe as provisional versus local-only?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
